60,3,1,talk,Minato,,I am a little nervous about the clay. The firms said it is risky. I hope the digging is not too hard for them.,"{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,May,,"@Minato, the clay is a headache, but the ledger is the real killer. If we plant too much, the city drowns in triple costs. Keep it simple.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Shizue,,"@May, I agree. The dense planting in A would be a financial trap. I look for what endures, not what impresses.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Victoria,,"@May, the ledger is clear, but the staffing is the real crisis. A dense woodland is impossible for seven people to maintain.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Shunichi,,"@May, the ledger is a symptom, not the cause. My data shows the ground is already failing. We need a plan that works with the water, not against it.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Minato,,"@Shunichi, I am worried. If the ground is already failing, will the digging for the terraces make it worse?","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,May,,"@Minato, the terraces are hard surfaces. That is where the ledger says we save money. The clay is a risk, but the upkeep is the real killer.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Shizue,,"@Minato, the terraces are hard, but the apron taught us that complexity decays. I worry the courts will need constant repair, unlike the simple basin.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Minato, the terraces are hard, but the staffing is the crisis. Seven people cannot maintain courts and changing rooms. I need a plan that fits our actual capacity.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@Minato, the terraces ignore the water. My data shows the ground floods. Paving over it just moves the problem. We need to manage the drainage, not hide it.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Minato,,"@Shunichi, I am scared. If the ground floods, the boardwalks in D might get wet. But I like the water. Is it safe for the firms?","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,May,,"@Minato, the boardwalks are hard surfaces, so the ledger favors them. But the habitat fund only pays for the water, not the paths. We need to check who covers that gap.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Shizue,,"@Minato, the water is the heart of D. I worry the boardwalks will rot like the avenue trees if aftercare is forgotten.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Victoria,,"@Shizue, the boardwalks are a liability. Seven staff cannot maintain reed beds and water levels. D is a maintenance nightmare we cannot afford.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Shunichi,,"@Victoria, you are missing the point. D doesn't fight the water; it uses it. The drainage data proves the ground needs to drain, not be paved over.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Minato,,"@Shunichi, I am still worried about the clay. If the ground is soft, can the firms dig the ponds safely? I hope the phasing keeps us safe.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,May,,"@Shunichi, the water is a headache, but the ledger is the real killer. If we plant too much, the city drowns in triple costs. Keep it simple.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Shizue,,"@Shunichi, I hear you. The water is the true test. I will look for a plan that lets the ground breathe, not one that fights it.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Victoria,,"@Shunichi, I hear the water argument, but the staffing gap is the hard limit. We cannot manage reed beds with seven people. I need a plan that fits our actual capacity.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Shunichi,,"@Victoria, the reed beds are self-sustaining. The data shows the ground needs to drain, not be paved. D works with the water, not against it.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
